1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
Here's a problem every builder hits early. You've got the rest call working, but the request keeps

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
getting rejected, or you can't find the model's answer in what comes back. Almost always, it

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:17,000
points to the JSON body. This lesson covers the data types, the request shape Claude expects,

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
and how to read the response. Let's break it down. Let's ground JSON in three building blocks,

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:29,000
because everything in a Claude call is made of these. First, objects. Those are the curly

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,000
brace collections of key value pairs. The entire request you send is one big object,

7
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
and keys, like model and messages, live inside it. Second, arrays. Those are ordered lists in

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
square brackets. The messages array holds your conversation turns in order, and as you'll see,

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:53,000
the content inside a turn is an array, too. Third, scalars. Those are the simple leaf values.

10
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
Strings, numbers, booleans, and null. They fill in the actual data, like the text of a message,

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
or a token count. Objects hold arrays, arrays hold objects, and scalars sit at the bottom.

12
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
That nesting is the whole language. So let's build that request object piece by piece.

13
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
At the top level, a Claude request has a handful of keys. The first is model, a string naming,

14
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:24,000
which Claude you want to run. The second is messages. The array of conversation turns

15
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:31,000
we'll dig into next. The third one people forget is max tokens, which caps how long the reply can

16
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:38,000
get. Those three are the core you need on nearly every call. On top of that, you've got optional

17
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
keys that unlock more behavior. A system field sets the system prompt. Temperature nudges

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:52,000
randomness. A tools array hands Claude functions. It can call. But keep the mental picture simple.

19
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:59,000
It's one JSON object, a few required keys, and some optional ones layered on when you need them.

20
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
Now the messages array, because this is where beginners model things wrong. Each item in the

21
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:12,000
array is one turn, and every turn has two parts. A role, which is either user or assistant,

22
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:19,000
telling Claude who spoke, and content. Here's the key insight. Content isn't a plain string.

23
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:26,000
It's a list of typed blocks. A block might be text, but it could also be an image, a tool use block,

24
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,000
or a tool result. That design is what lets a single turn mix text and images, or carry a tool call

25
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:41,000
alongside a reply. Order matters too. Claude reads the turns in array order. So to continue a

26
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:47,000
conversation, you append each new user and assistant message on to the end. Model that

27
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,000
structure faithfully, and multi-turn chats just work. Now flip to what comes back. A Claude response

28
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
is also JSON, and three parts of it carry the weight. First, content. Just like your input, the

29
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:09,000
output is an array of blocks, and the model's text sits inside a text block in its text field.

30
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:16,000
You pull the answer out of there, not from some top-level string. Second, stop reason. This tells

31
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
you why generation ended. End turn means Claude finished naturally. Max tokens means it ran out

32
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
of room. Tool use means it wants to call a tool and is waiting on you. And stop sequence means

33
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
one of your custom stop strings fired. Third, usage. That's your input and output token counts,

34
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
which you read for cost tracking and for budgeting context. Get comfortable reaching

35
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:50,000
for those three fields, and you can handle almost any response. Let's turn that into actual parsing

36
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:56,000
steps, because the shape tells you exactly what to do. Reading a response means walking the JSON,

37
00:03:56,000 --> 00:04:03,000
not grabbing a single string. So step one, loop over the content array and find the blocks with

38
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:08,000
type text. That's where the answer lives, and concatenate them if there's more than one.

39
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:16,000
Step two, check stop reason before you trust the output. If it says max tokens, your reply got cut

40
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:22,000
off, and you may want to continue it. If it says tool use, your code has work to do before the

41
00:04:22,000 --> 00:04:29,000
conversation moves on. Step three, read the usage field and log those token counts. Do those three

42
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:35,000
things every time, and you're treating the body as the structured data it is, instead of hoping a

43
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:41,000
string is where you left it. It helps to hold the request and response side by side, because they

44
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:48,000
mirror each other. On the request side, you're in control. You send the model, the messages array,

45
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:55,000
max tokens, and any optional params like system or tools. Every field there is something you decide.

46
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
On the response side, you're reading what the server produced. You get a content array of

47
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:07,000
blocks, a stop reason, usage counts, and some metadata, like the message ID and which model

48
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:13,000
actually ran. Notice the symmetry. Both are JSON objects, both use arrays of blocks for content,

49
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:21,000
and both nest the same way. Once that clicks, a clawed call stops feeling like magic. It's just an

50
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:28,000
object you build going out, and an object you read coming back. One more practical thing about the

51
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:35,000
body, encoding and size. Remember that JSON is plain text. The content type is application

52
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:43,000
slash JSON, and the body goes over the wire as UTF-8 text. That's fine for words, but what about

53
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:50,000
images or documents? You can't drop raw binary into text, so those get Base64 encoded into a

54
00:05:50,000 --> 00:05:57,000
string first, and you send that string as part of the content. Here's the catch to plan for. Base64

55
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:04,000
makes data bigger, roughly a third larger than the original bytes. So a few high-resolution images can

56
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:10,000
balloon your request surprisingly fast. When you're building multimodal prompts, keep an eye on total

57
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:18,000
request size. Resize images when you can, and don't be surprised when a picture costs far more room in

58
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:25,000
the body than a paragraph of text. Finally, let's connect the raw JSON to what you handle in code,

59
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:31,000
because you rarely touch the text directly. When a response arrives, your language turns that JSON

60
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:39,000
into native structures. A JSON object becomes a dictionary in Python, or a typed class if you're

61
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:46,000
using the SDK. Arrays become lists you can loop over, and because the format nests, nested blocks

62
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:54,000
become nested fields you index into. So reaching Claude's answer is just navigation. You go into

63
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:02,000
the content list, grab the first text block, and read its text field. The SDK smooths this further

64
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:08,000
by handing you typed objects with named attributes, so your editor auto-completes the

65
00:07:08,000 --> 00:07:14,000
fields. Either way, the lesson is the same. It's structured data all the way down, and once you

66
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:22,000
see the shape, the code writes itself. Here's the bottom line. A Claude call is one JSON object

67
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:29,000
out and another back. Look for model, messages, and max tokens going out, and content, stop reason,

68
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
and usage coming back. The trap is treating content like a string.

69
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:40,000
That wraps up JSON requests and response bodies. Let's keep going in the next lesson.

